Provisional result: no material algebraic defect found in the induction or leading-coefficient argument. This is an exposed same-model adversarial reconstruction, not blind independence. This reviewer authored the earlier static-bias transfer and bus proposals, so this pass is not independent validation of those parent proofs.

1. With omega greater than old range r, every plus-to-minus cross gap exceeds r. Every old positive gap occurs twice within the sign sectors. Within cross gaps only the d diagonal pairs repeat 2omega. Distinct old positive gaps imply uniqueness of all nonzero signed differences. These exhaust the repeated classes.
2. Reconstructed second-order coefficient d_(a,sigma)=sum_b |B_ab|^2/(E_a-E_b+2sigma omega). Both repeated classes split by D_a-D_b. The diagonal summand is required. For d>=3, an old pair {a,c}, c different from a,b, gives an uncancelled pole of D_a-D_b at |E_a-E_c|/2, residue |B_ac|^2/2. Strong old gaps exclude any row-b pole there. In the actual d=2 base the diagonal squares 4 and 0 give 4/omega. Equal diagonal squares would invalidate that special argument.
3. Recomputed contour estimates: radius gamma/3; perturbation ratio <=3/16; resolvent bound 48/(13gamma), projector norm <=16/13, and projector displacement <=3/13. The nonnormal complex perturbation is controlled by normal H0's resolvent. Its enclosed branch lies in its own beta|g| disk. Conjugation by I tensor Z makes each branch even. Cauchy therefore gives a quartic remainder bounded by 2beta|g|^4/rho^3. All stated thresholds leave strict margins.
4. Reconstructed both first-order transition slopes using bra conjugates: same-sign B_ab*4sigma omega/(4omega^2-Delta^2), opposite-sign -2sigma B_ab/(Delta+2sigma omega). The identity component cancels from the derivative. At equal old label the opposite-sign zeroth entry is one. For h_ij=<i|P_i O P_j|j>, the same slopes hold without nonholomorphic normalized vectors. Real projector overlaps stay nonzero. The Cauchy tails 8o g^2/rho^2 and 8o|g|/rho justify both transition thresholds.
5. At each finite induction stage the strict lower bounds and rational choices can be found without testing zero at a bad candidate. The rational real-symmetric Hamiltonian preserves the induction hypotheses; the endpoint operator remains I+X+Z.
6. The positive edge block has exact extreme eigenvalues 2-sqrt(2),4+sqrt(2). Its onsite I tensor X contribution is paid inside that old-edge block. The scalar reserve R_n and the sum of both incident edge norms in J_C are correctly counted. Access to all old path sites/edges, an endpoint port, and an occupied Hamiltonian path are explicit additional premises.
7. The complex Hilbert-Schmidt Gram has degree at most D=m(m+1). Its coefficient at D is exactly the Gram of the leading columns ad_C^(j+1)(B), positive by the full signed-gap Vandermonde. No mixed H/C word reaches this degree. Arbitrary noncommuting H causes no cancellation of this coefficient. Positivity is equivalent to all three strong conditions by adjoint-eigenspace rank. Real Hermitian rank is not being substituted for complex rank.
8. The D+1 rational-node search terminates by nonzero polynomial degree and nonnegativity, without exact equality tests. For arbitrary Hermitian H this is a spectral statement; physical bus composition also requires its parent's locality, full geometry, calibration and state premises. No later padding extension is imported.

